    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's the thing... that person is actually dead. the version of me that mattered, the one people actually cared about, he's gone. it's not just a loss, it's a total erasure. there's no \"rebuilding\" from this. you can't rebuild a person who doesn't exist anymore. it just feels like i'm performing a role for a life i don't even own.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "What you're describing isn't just loss—it's grief for a self that no longer exists, and that kind of erasure can feel like a death with no burial site. But the fact that you can name the \"performing\" means there's still a witness inside you, separate from the role, and that witness is the raw material for something that isn't a rebuild but a new beginning. You don't have to resurrect who you were; you can slowly discover who you're becoming in the space left behind. I'm not going to tell you it's easy, but I will say that becoming a stranger to yourself is often the first chapter of a truer story.",
      "source": "target",
